Composing functions.

The real leverage of functions-as-values is that you can combine small ones into bigger ones. Rather than write one big lambda, you build a pipeline from named pieces. And the standard functional interfaces ship with default methods for exactly this — composition is why they carry more than their single abstract method.

Start with function, which composes two ways: and-then, and compose. The only thing to get straight is the order. Say times-two is n, arrow, n times two, and plus-one is n, arrow, n plus one. Times-two dot and-then plus-one, applied to ten, gives twenty-one — times-two runs first, then plus-one: ten times two is twenty, plus one is twenty-one. But times-two dot compose plus-one, applied to ten, gives twenty-two — plus-one runs first, then times-two: ten plus one is eleven, times two is twenty-two. So f dot and-then g runs f, then feeds its result to g, reading left-to-right, in the intuitive order. And f dot compose g runs g first, then f — the mathematical f-of-g-of-x order. When in doubt, use and-then, because it reads in execution order.

Predicates combine with boolean logic, so you build a complex test from simple ones, without a tangled expression. Say non-blank tests that a string isn't blank, and shortish tests that it's under twenty characters. Then non-blank dot and shortish is a predicate where both must hold. Valid dot negate is the logical not. And non-blank dot or shortish holds when at least one does. And list dot remove-if, of non-blank dot negate, removes the blanks. Each of these returns a new predicate, leaving the originals untouched — small, reusable, named tests that you assemble right at the point of use.

Consumers chain too, with and-then, running one after another on the same input — handy for "do several things with each element." So a log consumer, that prints the order id, and a save consumer, that saves to a repository, combine as log dot and-then save; then orders dot for-each of that logs each order, then saves each.

And you've already met one of these: module four's comparator builders are this same idea. Comparing, then-comparing, reversed, composes a multi-key ordering out of single-key parts. Seeing it here reframes it — a comparator is a function, and then-comparing and reversed are its composition operators. So sorting people by last name, then-comparing first name, is composition.

So why compose at all? Three reasons. Readability — validate, and-then normalize, and-then save, states a whole pipeline as a sentence. Reuse — non-blank, shortish, times-two are each defined once, and combined many ways. And testability — each small function is trivially testable on its own, and the composite inherits their correctness.

And this is the mental model that makes streams click, in module seven: a stream pipeline is composed functions, with data flowing through — filter, with a predicate; map, with a function; for-each, with a consumer. Composition is the bridge from "a function is a value" to "a program is a pipeline." Next: methods that take and return functions — the higher-order methods that make composition a first-class tool in your own APIs.
